WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.000
God rest ye merry gentlemen, let nothing you dismay.

00:08.000 --> 00:16.500
Remember Christ our Savior was born on Christmas day

00:16.500 --> 00:25.000
to save us all from Satan's power when we were gone astray.

00:25.000 --> 00:34.000
Oh tidings of comfort and joy, comfort and joy.

00:34.000 --> 00:42.000
Oh tidings of comfort and joy.

00:42.000 --> 00:51.500
From God our heavenly Father, a blessed angel came

00:51.500 --> 01:00.500
and unto certain shepherds brought tidings of the same.

01:00.500 --> 01:09.500
A bird in Bethlehem was born, the Son of God by name.

01:09.500 --> 01:18.500
Oh tidings of comfort and joy, comfort and joy.

01:18.500 --> 01:26.500
Oh tidings of comfort and joy.

01:26.500 --> 01:35.500
Fear not then said the angel, let nothing you affright.

01:35.500 --> 01:44.500
This day is born a Savior of a pure virgin bride.

01:44.500 --> 01:59.500
To free our souls who trust in him.

01:59.500 --> 02:01.500
Gilbert K Chesterton once said,

02:01.500 --> 02:05.500
in everybody there is a thing that loves children,

02:05.500 --> 02:08.500
fears death, and likes sunlight.

02:08.500 --> 02:12.500
And this thing enjoys Charles Dickens.

02:12.500 --> 02:15.500
Before I tell you about the Christmas Carol,

02:15.500 --> 02:20.500
let me read to you what Charles Dickens himself wrote about this story.

02:20.500 --> 02:26.500
I have endeavored in this ghostly little story to raise the ghost of an idea

02:26.500 --> 02:29.500
which shall not put my readers out of humor with themselves,

02:29.500 --> 02:33.500
with each other, with the season, or with me.

02:33.500 --> 02:38.500
May it haunt their houses pleasantly. Charles Dickens.

02:38.500 --> 02:44.500
To begin with, Jacob Marley was dead. There is no doubt about that.

02:44.500 --> 02:47.500
You will therefore permit me to repeat emphatically

02:47.500 --> 02:50.500
that Marley was as dead as a doornail.

02:50.500 --> 02:53.500
Did Scrooge know that he was dead? Of course he did.

02:53.500 --> 02:58.500
Ebenezer Scrooge and he had been partners for I don't know how many years.

02:58.500 --> 03:01.500
Scrooge never painted out Marley's name,

03:01.500 --> 03:06.500
and so there it stood seven years afterwards, above the warehouse door.

03:06.500 --> 03:11.500
Ebenezer Scrooge. Oh, but he was a tight-fisted old sinner,

03:11.500 --> 03:14.500
hard and sharp as flint.

03:14.500 --> 03:19.500
The cold within him froze his features because he always carried his own low temperature with him.

03:19.500 --> 03:25.500
And he didn't fall one degree, not even at Christmas.

03:25.500 --> 03:30.500
Merry Christmas.

03:30.500 --> 03:33.500
Merry Christmas, Uncle. God save you.

03:33.500 --> 03:35.500
Christmas. Humbug.

03:35.500 --> 03:38.500
Christmas? A humbug? Uncle, you don't mean that, I'm sure.

03:38.500 --> 03:42.500
What reason have you to be merry? You're poor enough.

03:42.500 --> 03:44.500
What right have you to be dismal? You're rich enough.

03:44.500 --> 03:50.500
Humbug. What's Christmas to you but a time for finding yourself a year older,

03:50.500 --> 03:52.500
but not an hour richer?

03:52.500 --> 03:59.500
Every idiot that goes about with merry Christmas on his lips should be boiled in his own pudding.

03:59.500 --> 04:01.500
Now, Uncle, I know you're...

04:01.500 --> 04:05.500
Now, you keep Christmas in your own way, Fred, and I keep it in mine.

04:05.500 --> 04:08.500
Be off now. This is a place of business.

04:08.500 --> 04:10.500
Don't be angry. Come dine with us tomorrow.

04:10.500 --> 04:11.500
No.

04:11.500 --> 04:13.500
Caroline will be happy to see you.

04:13.500 --> 04:16.500
Why did you ever get married?

04:16.500 --> 04:18.500
Because I fell in love.

04:18.500 --> 04:19.500
Love?

04:19.500 --> 04:23.500
As the only thing in the world more ridiculous than a merry Christmas.

04:23.500 --> 04:25.500
Good afternoon, Neville.

04:25.500 --> 04:28.500
Uncle, I'm sorry with all my heart to find you like this.

04:28.500 --> 04:29.500
Merry Christmas, Bob.

04:29.500 --> 04:31.500
Merry Christmas, sir.

04:31.500 --> 04:49.500
Let me hear another sound from you, and you'll keep your Christmas by losing your situation.

04:49.500 --> 05:03.500
Uh, you'll, uh... you'll want all day tomorrow, I suppose, Cressie.

05:03.500 --> 05:05.500
Quite convenient, huh?

05:05.500 --> 05:06.500
It's not convenient.

05:06.500 --> 05:08.500
But it's only one day in the year, sir.

05:08.500 --> 05:14.500
A poor excuse to pick the pocket of your employer every 25th of December.

05:14.500 --> 05:16.500
But be here all year early next morning.

05:16.500 --> 05:17.500
I will, sir.

05:17.500 --> 05:20.500
And a merry Christmas to you, Mr. Scrooge.

05:20.500 --> 05:21.500
Ah!

05:31.500 --> 05:35.500
Christmas. Nonsense. Humbug.

05:35.500 --> 05:49.500
And while most of London was jovial and full of glee in honor of this Christmas Eve, Scrooge had taken his melancholy dinner at his usual melancholy tavern.

05:49.500 --> 05:54.500
And having read all the newspapers, he went home to bed.

05:54.500 --> 06:00.500
Scrooge lived in the chambers which had once belonged to Marley, his ex-partner.

06:00.500 --> 06:07.500
And as I have remarked before, Marley is dead these last seven years.

06:07.500 --> 06:36.500
Dead as a doornail.

06:36.500 --> 07:02.500
Mmm!

07:02.500 --> 07:08.500
I have been screwed.

07:08.500 --> 07:12.500
Oh no, what do you want of me?

07:12.500 --> 07:14.500
Who are you?

07:14.500 --> 07:19.500
In life I was your partner, Jacob Marley.

07:19.500 --> 07:22.500
You do not believe in me?

07:22.500 --> 07:24.500
I do not.

07:24.500 --> 07:29.500
What evidence would you have of my reality beyond that of your own senses?

07:29.500 --> 07:31.500
I do not know.

07:31.500 --> 07:34.500
Then why doubt your senses?

07:34.500 --> 07:39.500
Because a slight disorder in my stomach could make my senses cheat me.

07:39.500 --> 07:47.500
You might be an undigested bit of beef, a blot of mustard, a fragment of underdone potato.

07:47.500 --> 07:49.500
Humbug, I tell you, humbug.

07:49.500 --> 07:58.500
Oh, mercy on me, dreadful apparition.

07:58.500 --> 08:01.500
Now do you believe in me or not?

08:01.500 --> 08:03.500
I do, I do.

08:03.500 --> 08:07.500
But why do you come to plague me?

08:07.500 --> 08:12.500
And why do you wear that ponderous chain?

08:12.500 --> 08:18.500
I made it, link by link in my life, as you are doing for yourself on earth.

08:18.500 --> 08:21.500
It is now a part of my penance.

08:21.500 --> 08:26.500
And I am here tonight to warn you of a fate such as mine.

08:26.500 --> 08:33.500
But Ebenezer, if you change your ways, you have a chance of escaping my doom.

08:33.500 --> 08:36.500
You were always a good friend to me, Jacob.

08:36.500 --> 08:39.500
Thank you, thank you.

08:39.500 --> 08:42.500
You will be haunted by three spirits.

08:42.500 --> 08:47.500
Without their visits, you cannot hope to shun the path I tread.

08:47.500 --> 08:52.500
Expect the first tomorrow when the bell tolls one.

08:52.500 --> 08:57.500
Expect the next on the following night at the same hour.

08:57.500 --> 09:04.500
And the third on the next night when the last stroke of trail has ceased to vibrate.

09:04.500 --> 09:08.500
Couldn't I take them all at once, Jacob, and have it over with?

09:08.500 --> 09:11.500
Look to see me no more.

09:11.500 --> 09:14.500
And look to try your own sake.

09:14.500 --> 09:22.500
You remember what has passed between us.

09:44.500 --> 09:49.500
Scrooge tried desperately to say humbug to the strange happening,

09:49.500 --> 09:52.500
but the words stuck in his throat, unuttered.

09:52.500 --> 09:58.500
For it was highly probable it was not humbug.

09:58.500 --> 10:02.500
Being very much in need of repose from the experience he had undergone,

10:02.500 --> 10:05.500
or shall I say fatigues of the day,

10:05.500 --> 10:10.500
he went straight to bed without undressing and fell asleep in an instant.

10:10.500 --> 10:15.500
A few hours later.

10:15.500 --> 10:44.500
.

10:44.500 --> 10:49.500
Are you the spirit whose coming was foretold to me?

10:49.500 --> 10:50.500
I am.

10:50.500 --> 10:54.500
Who and what are you?

10:54.500 --> 10:57.500
I am the ghost of Christmas past.

10:57.500 --> 10:59.500
Long past?

10:59.500 --> 11:02.500
No, your past.

11:02.500 --> 11:05.500
What business brings you here?

11:05.500 --> 11:07.500
Your welfare.

11:07.500 --> 11:13.500
I have things to show you which are the shadow of the things that have been.

11:13.500 --> 11:19.500
Rise.

11:19.500 --> 11:32.500
And walk with me.

11:32.500 --> 11:39.500
The ghost of Christmas past led Scrooge down the road which he had forgotten for so many years.

11:39.500 --> 11:45.500
He showed him a Christmas day in the past which was a happy one for most children,

11:45.500 --> 11:48.500
but not for one lonely schoolboy.

11:48.500 --> 11:51.500
Ebenezer Scrooge.

11:51.500 --> 12:05.500
Do you know that boy?

12:05.500 --> 12:07.500
Yes, yes, I do.

12:07.500 --> 12:10.500
It is I as a boy.

12:10.500 --> 12:14.500
Oh, I remember that Christmas well.

12:14.500 --> 12:17.500
I felt so lonely.

12:17.500 --> 12:20.500
My playmates, they didn't like me.

12:20.500 --> 12:33.500
It was because you had shunned them.

12:33.500 --> 12:37.500
Oh, poor boy. I wish...

12:37.500 --> 12:39.500
What is the matter?

12:39.500 --> 12:43.500
There was a boy singing a Christmas carol on the street last night.

12:43.500 --> 12:48.500
I should have liked to have given him something, that's all.

12:48.500 --> 12:54.500
Shall I show you another Christmas forty years ago when a fair young girl released you from your marriage contract?

12:54.500 --> 12:55.500
No, no.

12:55.500 --> 12:58.500
Because she discovered you had ceased to love her.

12:58.500 --> 13:03.500
Your greed, avarice and desire for wealth had killed the love she had for you.

13:03.500 --> 13:09.500
No, no, spirit. Show me no more. Leave me. I want no longer.

13:09.500 --> 13:16.500
Awakening in the middle of a prodigiously tough snore and sitting up in bed to get his thoughts together,

13:16.500 --> 13:25.500
Scrooge had no occasion to be told that the bell was again upon the stroke of one.

13:25.500 --> 13:45.500
Scrooge!

13:56.500 --> 14:00.500
I am the ghost of Christmas presents. Look upon me.

14:00.500 --> 14:04.500
Yes, spirit. Conduct me where you will.

14:04.500 --> 14:10.500
I went forth last night on compulsion and I learned a lesson which is working now.

14:10.500 --> 14:16.500
Tonight, if you have ought to teach me, let me profit by it.

14:16.500 --> 14:29.500
Touch my robe.

14:29.500 --> 14:38.500
Look.

14:38.500 --> 14:40.500
Belinda, bring the plates.

14:40.500 --> 14:41.500
I'm not having any.

14:41.500 --> 14:44.500
Belinda, there's a great deal of work to be done. Help your sister.

14:44.500 --> 14:48.500
If I came by outside, I could smell the sage and the onion.

14:48.500 --> 14:49.500
You knew it was our tomorrow.

14:49.500 --> 14:50.500
Oh, I certainly did, boy.

14:50.500 --> 14:52.500
Peter, set the chairs.

14:52.500 --> 14:54.500
Can I put some checklist on the fire, mother?

14:54.500 --> 14:56.500
No, your father will do that when he comes.

14:56.500 --> 15:00.500
What has gotten your precious father and your brother time, is it?

15:00.500 --> 15:04.500
And Martha wasn't as late last year by half an hour.

15:04.500 --> 15:07.500
This is the house of your clerk, Bob Cratchit.

15:07.500 --> 15:10.500
But he has hardly a penny to his name.

15:10.500 --> 15:14.500
I, the ghost of Christmas present, have blessed his house.

15:14.500 --> 15:19.500
Here comes Bob now with another member of his family, Tiny Tim.

15:19.500 --> 15:21.500
And a Merry Christmas, everybody.

15:21.500 --> 15:24.500
Tiny Tim, Merry Christmas.

15:24.500 --> 15:27.500
And there you are, Tiny Tim, right in your own chair.

15:27.500 --> 15:28.500
Merry Christmas, Bob.

15:28.500 --> 15:29.500
And Martha.

15:29.500 --> 15:31.500
Yes, here I am again.

15:31.500 --> 15:34.500
Christmas wouldn't be Christmas without Martha coming to visit us.

15:34.500 --> 15:37.500
And how did Tiny Tim behave today, Bob?

15:37.500 --> 15:41.500
Fine, he told me coming home that he was glad he'd been to church.

15:41.500 --> 15:44.500
Because it's pleasant to remember that the day is called Christmas.

15:44.500 --> 15:48.500
After he who made the lame to walk and the blind to see.

15:48.500 --> 15:52.500
Oh, what a beautiful Christmas table.

15:52.500 --> 15:54.500
It would be more beautiful if we had a turkey.

15:54.500 --> 15:56.500
But we'll manage.

15:56.500 --> 15:58.500
Of course we will.

15:58.500 --> 16:02.500
See what a happy family your clerk has on only 15 shillings a week?

16:02.500 --> 16:04.500
Tiny Tim.

16:04.500 --> 16:09.500
I didn't know he was sick and a cripple.

16:09.500 --> 16:13.500
Mr. Scrooge, I give you Mr. Scrooge, the founder of the feast.

16:13.500 --> 16:15.500
The founder of the feast, indeed.

16:15.500 --> 16:16.500
I wish I had him here.

16:16.500 --> 16:18.500
I'd give him a piece of my mind to feast upon.

16:18.500 --> 16:20.500
Dear, it's Christmas Day.

16:20.500 --> 16:22.500
It should be Christmas Day, I'm sure.

16:22.500 --> 16:27.500
When one drinks the health of such an odious, stingy, hard, unfeeling man as Mr. Scrooge.

16:27.500 --> 16:29.500
My dear, it's Christmas.

16:29.500 --> 16:30.500
Very well.

16:30.500 --> 16:32.500
I'll drink his health for your sake.

16:32.500 --> 16:34.500
And because it's Christmas.

16:34.500 --> 16:36.500
Long life to him.

16:36.500 --> 16:38.500
A merry Christmas and a happy new year.

16:38.500 --> 16:41.500
A merry Christmas and a happy new year.

16:41.500 --> 16:44.500
God bless us, everyone.

16:50.500 --> 16:54.500
Tell me, Tiny Tim, will he live?

16:54.500 --> 16:58.500
I see a vacant seat and a little crutch without an owner.

16:58.500 --> 16:59.500
No, no.

16:59.500 --> 17:01.500
Say he will be spared.

17:01.500 --> 17:03.500
My life on the globe is very brief.

17:03.500 --> 17:04.500
It ends tonight.

17:04.500 --> 17:05.500
Tonight?

17:05.500 --> 17:09.500
The time is drawing near for me to go and for your third visitor to appear.

17:09.500 --> 17:16.500
I am in the presence of the Ghost of Christmas yet to come.

17:16.500 --> 17:20.500
Ghost of the future, I fear you more than any specter I have seen.

17:20.500 --> 17:22.500
But lead on.

17:22.500 --> 17:26.500
I want to know what you will do to me.

17:26.500 --> 17:30.500
I want to know what you will do to me.

17:30.500 --> 17:35.500
I want to know what you will do to me.

17:35.500 --> 17:39.500
I want to know what you will do to me.

17:39.500 --> 17:43.500
I want to know what you will do to me.

18:04.500 --> 18:07.500
I met a very kind young man today who asked after you.

18:07.500 --> 18:09.500
His nephew, whom I scarcely know.

18:09.500 --> 18:11.500
I told him about Tiny Tim and he said,

18:11.500 --> 18:14.500
I'm heartily sorry for you and your good wife.

18:14.500 --> 18:16.500
By the by, how he ever knew that, I don't know.

18:16.500 --> 18:17.500
Knew what, dear?

18:17.500 --> 18:18.500
That you were a good wife.

18:18.500 --> 18:20.500
Oh, everyone knows that.

18:20.500 --> 18:24.500
And he said, if I can be of any service to you at any time, pray come to me.

18:24.500 --> 18:28.500
He's rather unlike his old uncle Scrooge, isn't he?

18:32.500 --> 18:34.500
If you must torment me, be quick.

18:34.500 --> 18:37.500
Take me to what else you have to show.

18:47.500 --> 18:49.500
I don't know much about it either.

18:49.500 --> 18:50.500
I only know he's dead.

18:50.500 --> 18:51.500
When did he die?

18:51.500 --> 18:52.500
Last night, I believe.

18:52.500 --> 18:54.500
What did he done with his money?

18:54.500 --> 18:55.500
I haven't heard.

18:55.500 --> 18:57.500
But he'll have no use for it where he's going.

18:57.500 --> 18:59.500
It's likely to be a cheap funeral.

18:59.500 --> 19:02.500
For upon my life, I don't know anyone to go to it.

19:02.500 --> 19:05.500
Suppose we make up a party and volunteer.

19:05.500 --> 19:09.500
When I come to think of it, I'm not at all sure that I wasn't his most particular friend.

19:09.500 --> 19:12.500
For we used to stop and speak whenever we met.

19:12.500 --> 19:15.500
I don't mind going to his funeral if lunch is provided.

19:19.500 --> 19:21.500
Tell me what man they are speaking of.

19:21.500 --> 19:42.500
Who is it that lies dead?

19:42.500 --> 19:44.500
No, no, hear me.

19:44.500 --> 19:46.500
I am not the man I was.

19:46.500 --> 19:50.500
Assure me I yet may change the shadows you have shown me.

19:50.500 --> 19:55.500
I will honor Christmas in my heart and try to keep it all the year.

19:55.500 --> 20:06.500
Tell me I may sponge away the writing on this stone.

20:06.500 --> 20:08.500
Marley!

20:08.500 --> 20:10.500
Marley, Jacob, Marley!

20:10.500 --> 20:13.500
Heaven and Christmas time be praised for this.

20:13.500 --> 20:16.500
I will live in the past, the present, and the future.

20:16.500 --> 20:29.500
The spirit of all three shall thrive within me.

20:29.500 --> 20:30.500
Boy, oh boy!

20:30.500 --> 20:31.500
Yes sir?

20:31.500 --> 20:32.500
What's today?

20:32.500 --> 20:33.500
What sir?

20:33.500 --> 20:35.500
What's today my fine fellow?

20:35.500 --> 20:36.500
Today?

20:36.500 --> 20:38.500
By Christmas day sir.

20:38.500 --> 20:41.500
It's still Christmas and I haven't missed it.

20:41.500 --> 20:56.500
For a man who had been out of practice for years,

20:56.500 --> 20:59.500
Scrooge gave a most illustrious laugh,

20:59.500 --> 21:03.500
the father of a long line of brilliant laughs.

21:03.500 --> 21:23.500
And Scrooge dressed himself all in his best

21:23.500 --> 21:29.500
and at last got out into the streets wishing everyone he met a merry Christmas.

21:29.500 --> 21:30.500
Mr. Fred!

21:30.500 --> 21:33.500
Father Fred, have you room at your table for some friends?

21:33.500 --> 21:36.500
Oh, you're very welcome Mr. Fred, sir, and you too ma'am.

21:36.500 --> 21:38.500
I only hope that our lowly feast is to your liking.

21:38.500 --> 21:41.500
Now don't fret about provisions, we've brought plenty for all.

21:41.500 --> 21:43.500
Merry Christmas, Bob!

21:43.500 --> 21:44.500
Mr. Scrooge!

21:44.500 --> 21:46.500
And Mrs. Cratchit, for you.

21:46.500 --> 21:47.500
Mr. Scrooge!

21:47.500 --> 21:50.500
My dear, that's for you.

21:50.500 --> 21:52.500
Darling, for you.

21:52.500 --> 21:53.500
For me?

21:53.500 --> 21:54.500
And you, sir.

21:54.500 --> 21:57.500
Oh, Mr. Scrooge, I don't know how we can ever thank you.

21:57.500 --> 22:03.500
Don't, and I'm going to raise your salary and help your large family in every way possible.

22:03.500 --> 22:12.500
And, tiny Tim, I saw a friend of mine at church just a little while ago, a famous surgeon.

22:12.500 --> 22:18.500
You and I are going to see him tomorrow, and he's going to be your friend too.

22:18.500 --> 22:19.500
Mr. Scrooge!

22:19.500 --> 22:22.500
Merry Christmas, Mr. Scrooge!

22:22.500 --> 22:24.500
Merry Christmas!

22:24.500 --> 22:31.500
Scrooge, when his word, he did it all and infinitely more.

22:31.500 --> 22:37.500
And to tiny Tim, who did not die, he was a second father.

22:37.500 --> 22:44.500
Scrooge had no further dealings with ghosts, but it was always said that he knew how to keep Christmas well,

22:44.500 --> 22:48.500
if any man alive possessed the knowledge.

22:48.500 --> 22:52.500
May it truly be said of us, and all of us.

22:52.500 --> 22:58.500
And so, as tiny Tim observed, God bless us, everyone.

22:58.500 --> 23:25.500
The first Noel the angel did say, was to certain poor shepherds in fields as they lay, in fields as they lay, keeping their sheep on a cold winter's night that was so deep.

23:25.500 --> 23:41.500
Noel, Noel, Noel, Noel, born is the King of Israel.

23:41.500 --> 23:56.500
And there it is most often stayed, right over the place where Jesus lay.

23:56.500 --> 24:11.500
Noel, Noel, Noel, Noel, born is the King of Israel.

24:11.500 --> 24:26.500
Then entered in their white and green, the reverend's lead upon their knees,

24:26.500 --> 24:41.500
and offered there his presence, their gold and myrrh and frankincense.

24:41.500 --> 25:02.500
Noel, Noel, Noel, Noel, born is the King of Israel.

